#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ds3-texup: 4x the Archdragon Peak cloud sea and fog sprites, and repack the binders.

Fixes B and C of docs/research/ds3-graphics-modernisation-20260903.md. The
"outdated" look of that map is measurable, not vibes: the cloud sea is 1024 px
BC1 tiles stretched over a hundred metres, and the fog wisps are 8x8 flipbooks
whose individual frames are 128 px. This tool pulls those textures out of the
archives, upscales them, re-encodes them as BC7/BC4 with a full mip chain, and
PATCHES the binders they came from in place. It never touches the real install:
it reads through souls-extract's DvdBnd and writes to data/ds3-ui-port/build/texup/.

    tools/ds3-texup extract      archives -> build/texup/orig/*.png + manifest.json
    tools/ds3-texup upscale      orig     -> build/texup/up/*.png
    tools/ds3-texup encode       up       -> build/texup/dds/*.dds  (+ round-trip PSNR)
    tools/ds3-texup pack         dds      -> build/texup/mod-map/ and mod-sfx/
    tools/ds3-texup preview      original vs upscaled 1:1 crops
    tools/ds3-texup seamcheck    the flipbook frame-boundary proof
    tools/ds3-texup check FILE   structural check of any BND4/BXF4, ours or not
    tools/ds3-texup all          everything above, in order
    tools/ds3-texup report       the manifest as a table
    tools/ds3-texup deploy-cmds  print the cp commands (it does NOT deploy)

The map and sfx outputs are SEPARATE on purpose, so a capture can blame one of
them rather than both.

Flags: --only SUBSTR (restrict to matching texture names), --force (redo work
whose output already exists), --quality N (bc7e level 0..6, default 6).

MERGE FLAGS on `pack`, because ModEngine2 is first-match-wins per FILE and three
mods that each ship a whole map/m32 binder do not layer, they shadow:

    --base-dir DIR   use this pack's binders as the base (container included)
    --inject DIR     patch in every entry DIR's binders changed against the game
                     original (entry data only). Repeatable, later wins
    --out-name NAME  build subdirectory for the map output (default mod-map)
    --skip-sfx       map binders only

Built 3 Sep 2026 for Archdragon Peak, where three sources touch map/m32:

    tools/ds3-texup pack --skip-sfx --out-name mod-map-merged \
      --base-dir /mnt/disk1/ds3-mods/staging/ti \
      --inject   /mnt/disk1/ds3-mods/staging/visual-overhaul

WHY THESE TOOLS.

  Upscaler: realesrgan-ncnn-vulkan (~/src/realesrgan), model realesrgan-x4plus.
  It is a static Vulkan binary, so it runs on the RX 9070 XT through RADV with
  no PyTorch and no ROCm. Measured on this box: 1024x1024 -> 4096x4096 in 16.0 s
  whole-sheet, 5.7 s as 64 separate 128 px frames. The general x4plus model, not
  the anime one: clouds and smoke are photographic.

  MEASURED TRAP, and the reason this tool splits channels. Feed
  realesrgan-ncnn-vulkan an RGBA PNG and it runs the NETWORK on RGB only and
  BICUBIC-resizes the alpha. Proved on s50065 (a 64x64 alpha blob): its alpha
  output differs from a bicubic resize by mean 0.26/255 and from the network's
  own output on the same channel by 1.66/255. Every fog sprite here IS its alpha
  channel, so a naive RGBA run silently decides the most important channel with
  the cheapest filter in the binary.

  Splitting the channels then showed the network is the WRONG tool for that
  channel anyway: on a fog puff it hardens the soft edge and turns the feather
  into radial filaments (see `ai_upscale` for the three-way comparison). So RGB
  goes through the network and alpha gets LANCZOS, per frame. RGB is dilated into
  the transparent region first, so the network never drags whatever junk sits
  behind alpha=0 into a visible edge.

  Encoder: bc7enc_rdo (~/src/bc7enc_rdo, built here with bc7e.ispc for all BC7
  modes). "Nothing on Linux encodes BC7" is no longer true. Two build fixes were
  needed and are recorded in the Pipeline section of the research doc: ert.h
  needs <cstdint> on GCC 13+, and the ispc objects need --pic or the link fails
  with "relocation R_X86_64_32S ... can not be used when making a PIE object".
  Measured: 4096x4096 BC7 at bc7e quality 6 in 6.1 s wall (48 s of CPU across
  the pool), round-trip 52.2 dB against the source PNG.

  Compressonator was not tried: bc7enc built and ran in under a minute once the
  two fixes were in, and it is the encoder the RDO texture world actually uses.

WHY THE FORMAT CHOICES.

  The TPF entries hold COMPLETE DDS files, and DS3 reads the DDS header, not the
  TPF format byte. Proof from its own data: m32_00_sky_02_a and the stormsky set
  are TPF format 102 with DXGI 99 (BC7_UNORM_SRGB) while the sfx sprites are TPF
  format 102 with DXGI 98 (BC7_UNORM). One TPF code, two DXGI codes. So the rule
  here is: PRESERVE the original DDS header byte-for-byte and patch only
  width/height/mipMapCount/linearSize, plus the DXGI code when a format actually
  changes. Nothing else about the header is invented.

  The one format change is the cloud albedo. Those ship BC1_UNORM_SRGB (DXGI 72,
  TPF format 0/1), which is exactly what puts block artefacts on the soft
  gradients. They go to BC7_UNORM_SRGB (DXGI 99, TPF format 102), which is a
  combination DS3 already ships. Normals stay BC7 linear (DXGI 98, TPF 106/107),
  masks stay BC4 (FourCC ATI1, TPF 103).

  Uncompressed BGRA, the fallback replace_texture.py uses for UI atlases, is not
  an option at this scale: one 4096x4096 texture would be 64 MB before mips.

WHY MIPS ARE GENERATED HERE. Every original carries a full chain down to 1x1
(kumo_20_a: 11 levels, 699,212 bytes). bc7enc cannot make mips, so this tool
makes them itself and concatenates the encoded levels. Shipping a mip-less 4096
texture would alias worse at distance than the 1024 original looks up close,
which is the opposite of the point. sRGB albedo is downsampled in LINEAR light
(decode sRGB, resize, re-encode); linear data (normals, masks, sprite alpha) is
resized as-is.

WHY MASKS AND NORMALS DO NOT GET THE AI. A BC4 mask and a two-channel normal are
DATA, not a picture, and a hallucinating generator is exactly wrong for them.
They get LANCZOS. Normals are then clamped so x^2 + y^2 <= 1 in the tangent
plane (DS3 stores X in R and Y in G, B is a near-constant third channel and A is
unused: measured on kumo_20_n, R and G span 66..189 around 127 while B sits at
57..66), because LANCZOS overshoot can push a resampled normal off the unit
sphere. Honest note: a LANCZOS mask upscale buys interpolation smoothness and
nothing else. It is here because a 4x albedo against a 1x mask can beat on the
block edges of the mask; skip it with --only if you disagree.

WHY FLIPBOOKS ARE UPSCALED PER FRAME. A sprite sheet is 64 unrelated pictures in
a trench coat, and any filter with a footprint, the network or LANCZOS, will drag
frame N+1 into frame N's border if it is run over the whole sheet. Per frame is
also 2.8x faster here (5.7 s against 16.0 s for s11630), because the binary's
model load and Vulkan enumeration dominate a small tile. `seamcheck` measures the
difference between the two as a function of distance from a frame edge; the
numbers for s11630 are in the Pipeline section of the research doc. s11640 is the
exception: it is 8 columns x 5 ROWS in a 2048x1024 sheet, so its cell height is
204.8 px and there is no integer slice; it is upscaled whole.

SIZE CAP. 4x, then capped at 4096 on the long side, so a 2048 source gets 2x and
kumo_50_m (already 4096x1024) is skipped entirely. The one exception is the sky
panorama m32_00_sky_02_a, 4096x2048, which is allowed 8192x4096 because it is
the single texture wrapped round the whole sky and is therefore the most
stretched thing on screen. It is produced by a 4x pass followed by a LANCZOS
halving, which reads more detail out of the source than a native 2x pass would.

VERIFICATION, and why the first version's verification was worthless. `encode`
decodes every finished DDS back through tpf_png.decode and reports PSNR against
the PNG it came from. That part was always fine.

The binder part was not. Version 1 RE-SERIALISED the binders with soulstruct,
proved it by reloading them with soulstruct, and shipped something that made the
game draw the wrong textures on the Archdragon Peak arch, pillars and floor,
white geometry chunks, and the fog as solid white slabs. A library re-parsing its
own output proves only that the library is self-consistent. Two faults, both
invisible to that check and both found with the reader in this file:

  1. BND4's hash table stores `path_hashes_offset` as an ABSOLUTE FILE OFFSET,
     and the table maps a path hash to an ENTRY INDEX. The re-serialised BHD came
     out 2 bytes shorter, because soulstruct does not pad the UTF-16 name block
     to 8 before the table, so the table moved to 23030 while its own header
     still said 23032. Every lookup read the hash array 2 bytes skewed and the
     last record ran off the end of the file.
  2. soulstruct writes 10 pad bytes between entry data blocks, which destroys the
     16-byte alignment the format requires: 228 of 245 entry offsets came out
     misaligned, against 245 of 245 in the shipped file.

So `pack` no longer serialises anything. It appends the new payloads to the end
of the original bytes, 16-byte aligned, and rewrites ONLY the three fields of the
replaced entries' own records (compressed size, uncompressed size, data offset).
Everything else in both files keeps its bytes AND its file position. `_verify`
then checks that with our own reader: the header, name block and hash table are
untouched and in place, every path hash still resolves to an entry whose name
hashes to it, every entry is 16-byte aligned, every untouched record is
byte-identical, and the byte ranges that did change all lie inside a replaced
record. `check` runs the same structural test on any binder, so the shipped file
and a third-party pack that is known to work in game can be used as controls.
"""
